\f1\fs22 Johnny jumped to his feet. \'93Eloise! What are you doing here?\'94\
\'93Protecting my investments.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2  Sarah sat at her workstation willing herself to breathe evenly.\
\'93You should be in the bunker,\'94 Eloise told Sarah. \'93In bed. In a nightie. With fluffy socks on.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Sarah took a sharp breath and winced. \'93It\'92s no longer secure,\'94 she said. \'93And the children got up early to play with their new toys.\'94 She glanced at Johnny.\
\'93I\'92d still challenge anyone to find the bunker,\'94 Eloise said flatly, then asked for a situation report.\
Johnny and Chip tried to override each other with their versions of the last hour or so. Eloise waved them both down and deferred to Ortolan. He recounted the unsolicited software upgrade, Johnny\'92s subscription, the diagnostics still in progress.\
Eloise held her hand up to Chip when he tried to give his version.\
\'93Tell me about the diagnostics,\'94 Eloise said.\
Ortolan explained how the hardware was monitored by a software harness that stimulated low-level functions and verified its state, especially that it remained within acceptable tolerances. There were also tests for the applications that took commands from, and reported back results to the workstations. And there were tests for the software embedded in the Washing Machine, which would exercise the new GygesOS Daintree version 42.42.42 itself.\
Chip wanted to interrupt again.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \'93Presumably these tests will verify whether the new version behaves the same way the previous one did,\'94 Eloise said.\
Ortolan nodded.\
\'93And that it does nothing the old firmware didn\'92t?\'94\
He hesitated.\
\'93That\'92s rather different, isn't it?\'94 Eloise said and watched Chip relax\'85 a little. \'93If the new version does more than the tests are designed to verify, those extra things would slide on past, wouldn\'92t they?\'94\
\'93They would, yes,\'94 Ortolan conceded.\
\'93It could be spying on us,\'94 Eloise offered.\
Ortolan weighed that. \'93It could collect extra data, but we\'92d catch it if tried to transmit it anywhere.\'94\
\'93Anywhere conventional,\'94 Chip challenged. \'93We don\'92t even know how the new version arrived.\'94\
Ortolan turned to the workstation he\'92d been studying. \'93The diagnostics have finished.\'94\
Johnny put his device away. \'93No complaints?\'94\
Ortolan scanned the screen, scrolled and made a few other gestures to pop and close windows. \'93If anything, the data suggest better performance; smoother running; potentially stronger, more stable multi-dimensional field generation.\'94\
Johnny rocked on his toes.\'93We could run it faster safely.\'94\
\'93That increases the danger of seismic activity,\'94 Chip warned.\
\'93We should test that,\'94 Johnny said. \'93Give it a run.\'94\
Eloise nodded. \'93I agree.\'94\
\'93Test what?\'94 Sarah demanded. \'93That we could cause more havoc?\'94\
\'93It wouldn\'92t be havoc if we control it,\'94 Johnny said.\
\'93You want to control earthquakes,\'94 Sarah accused. \'93After all this bull-tax about communicating with other realities\'85\'94 Sarah shook her head.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \'93Well.\'94 Eloise clapped once. \'93Let\'92s take her for a spin.\'94\
\'93No!\'94 Sarah raised her voice and was immediately rewarded with a crunch in her abdomen.\
Eloise saw Sarah\'92s discomfort while ignoring her objection. \'93Merry Christmas, everyone! Engage; hit it, or whatever you geeks say on this bridge.\'94\
Sarah\'92s eyes narrowed. \'93I believe the command you\'92re looking for is, \'91warp me\'92.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Sarah held her stare; Eloise let her.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Chip looked to the floor and shook his head. One of his legs wanted to take him to throttle Johnny\'92s puny neck. The other wanted to take him to the door.\
Sarah lifted herself to her feet. \'93Eloise, no. Let\'92s pause\'85 we need to dump the code and reverse-engineer it\'85 at least find out what else is in there that our diagnostics don\'92t reach.\'94\
Eloise remained stony-faced.\
\'93Eloise, it\'92s Christmas. Let\'92s take a break, come back in the new year. What\'92s the hurry?\'94\
\'93You seem to have forgotten we\'92re bunkering underground while there\'92s a mutiny on the surface. This 
\f2\i is 
\f1\i0 my Christmas.\'94\
Sarah chewed over a thought. \'93I\'92m going to the townhouse to gather my things. Then I\'92m leaving. Obviously I\'92ll be taking Little Clinton.\'94 She patted her belly. \'93And I\'92ll be divorcing Alexander. Looks like you\'92ll need a new succession plan.\'94 Sarah made for the door.\
\'93You think I will just let you leave? The compound is on lock-down.\'94\
Sarah swung on Eloise. \'93Tony will fly me out of here. The Elvi will help. Tax, apparently Tony has clones of Agent Jones as bodyguards.\'94\
\'93The Jones clones will turn on Chantara,\'94 Eloise said.\
\'93Tony can make friends with anyone.\'94\
Eloise paused. \'93I\'92ll shoot you down.\'94\
Sarah sneered. \'93Will you? If you shoot down a Hardy Industries hover-copt, every oligarch in the country\'97hell, in the whole world\'97will declare war on you. I doubt even you could survive that. Now get out of my way.\'94\
As convincingly as a heavily pregnant woman could storm out, Sarah played the part. Chip opened the door for her.\
Sarah left. Before Chip had closed the door, Eloise called out to her, threw the door out of Chip\'92s hands and followed.
